  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nUpper level low will continue to push east of the forecast area as a \nsurface ridge builds overhead from the west tonight. Currently, lake \neffect rain showers persist across portions of Northeast Ohio and \nNorthwest Pennsylvania. Anticipate for shower coverage to diminish \nfrom west to east with the arrival of the surface ridge tonight. \nThere will be a window of dry weather and clearing with the surface \nridge before another upper level trough clips the northeastern \nportions of the forecast area on Sunday night. Lake effect rain \nshowers will redevelop across the traditional Snowbelt region Sunday \nnight into Monday before diminishing through the day on Monday. \n\nNorthwest winds this afternoon and evening will continue to \noccasionally gust to 20-25 MPH before the ridge enters from the \nwest. The pressure gradient tightens as the trough approaches the \nregion which will lead to westerly wind gusts increasing to 25-30 \nMPH by Sunday.  \n\nRemaining cooler this weekend with low temperatures tonight falling \nto the upper 20s to lower 30s under a mainly clear sky. High \ntemperatures on Sunday warm into the mid to upper 50s before lows \nSunday night dip into the mid to upper 30s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe first half of the week looks mainly dry and mild as surface\nhigh pressure remains across the Ohio Valley. Another upper \ntrough will dig south into the region later in the week. \nHowever, current medium range models have some differences in \nexact timing. For now, expect for unsettled weather to return at\nsome point later in the week with precipitation chances \nincreasing Thursday onward. The upper trough will also introduce\na cold airmass with 850mb temperatures of -5 to -10C, depending\non what model you look at, which may usher in our first snow or\nrain/snow mix of the season.",
